+# Forking Base locally
+
+Our contract reads USDC on Base at `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913`, so the
+local node has to be a fork of **Base**, not of whatever chain the default happens to be.
+
+## What the onboarding note gets wrong
+
+The note says two things, and both are wrong.
+
+### 1. `yarn fork base` does not fork Base — it forks Ethereum mainnet
+
+The root `package.json` forwards `fork` to the foundry workspace:
+
+```json
+"fork": "yarn foundry:fork",
+"foundry:fork": "yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork",
+```
+
+and `packages/foundry/package.json` defines it as:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+That `${1:-mainnet}` never sees your argument. Yarn 4 (`packageManager: yarn@4.13.0`)
+does not bind CLI arguments to positional parameters in `scripts`; a script that
+references `$1` gets `Unbound argument #1`, and `${1:-mainnet}` therefore always falls
+back to `mainnet`. Verified in this checkout with a throwaway script:
+
+```
+$ yarn t1 base        # script body: echo FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet} END
+FORK_URL=mainnet END
+$ yarn t2 base        # script body: echo ONE=$1 END
+Unbound argument #1
+```
+
+So `yarn fork base` runs exactly what `yarn fork` runs. The `base` is silently discarded.
+
+`packages/foundry/Makefile`:
+
+```make
+# Start a fork
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+With `FORK_URL=mainnet`, anvil resolves the alias from the `[rpc_endpoints]` table in
+`packages/foundry/foundry.toml`:
+
+```toml
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+base    = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+i.e. Ethereum mainnet. **That is why the token reads come back empty.** Nothing is
+deployed at `0x8335…2913` on Ethereum mainnet, so `eth_call` against it returns `0x` and
+every read decodes to zero/empty. The node is healthy, transactions mine, the contract is
+fine — it is just pointed at the wrong chain. Confirmed against a real mainnet fork:
+
+```
+$ cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 --rpc-url <mainnet-fork>
+0x
+$ cast call 0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48 "symbol()(string)" --rpc-url <mainnet-fork>
+"USDC"     # mainnet's USDC is there; Base's is not
+```
+
+(Side note: the shared demo `ALCHEMY_API_KEY=IZYEU2cWBgnFmgiTAgpWD` in
+`packages/foundry/.env` is frequently rate-limited — while writing this, forking `mainnet`
+through it failed outright with `Error: failed to get fork block number`. Another reason
+not to rely on the `mainnet` default.)
+
+### 2. `cast chain-id` cannot tell you which chain you forked
+
+The Makefile passes `--chain-id 31337` unconditionally. That is deliberate — the frontend
+targets `chains.foundry` (`packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts`: `targetNetworks: [chains.foundry]`),
+which is chain id 31337 — so **do not remove it**. But it means the fork always reports
+31337 no matter what it forked. Measured on two real forks, both started by the Makefile
+recipe:
+
+| fork of | `cast chain-id` | `cast code 0x8335…2913` |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Base (`https://mainnet.base.org`) | `31337` | non-empty |
+| Ethereum mainnet | `31337` | `0x` |
+
+`cast chain-id` returning a number only proves *a node is listening*. The teammate who ran
+it got a true answer to a question that has the same answer on every chain, and concluded
+the fork was healthy.
+
+## The command that actually forks Base here
+
+From the repo root:
+
+```bash
+yarn workspace @se-2/foundry exec make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+or equivalently from `packages/foundry`:
+
+```bash
+yarn exec make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+`FORK_URL` must be set as a make variable — it is the only thing the recipe reads. `base`
+resolves through `foundry.toml`'s `[rpc_endpoints]` to `https://mainnet.base.org`; you can
+also pass a full URL (`FORK_URL=https://…`) if you want your own provider.
+
+Use `yarn exec` / run from inside the workspace rather than bare `make`: the
+`setup-anvil-wallet` prerequisite calls `shx`, which lives in `node_modules/.bin` and is
+only on `PATH` under yarn. Bare `make fork FORK_URL=base` fails with
+`/bin/sh: shx: command not found`.
+
+Verified — the command reaches anvil with the right alias:
+
+```
+$ yarn workspace @se-2/foundry exec make fork FORK_URL=base
+…
+`scaffold-eth-default` keystore was saved successfully. Address: 0xa0ee7a142d267c1f36714e4a8f75612f20a79720
+anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+Plain `yarn fork` (no argument) still forks Ethereum mainnet. If we want `yarn fork base`
+to work as the note assumed, the script has to stop using `$1`, e.g. change
+`packages/foundry/package.json` to `"fork": "make fork"` and invoke it as
+`yarn fork FORK_URL=base` — trailing args *are* appended when the script body has no
+positional reference (`yarn t2 base` printed `B base`).
+
+## The check that would have caught this
+
+Ask the node something only Base can answer. Read the token you actually depend on:
+
+```bash
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "symbol()(string)" --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+- fork of Base → `"USDC"`
+- fork of anything else → `Error: contract 0x8335… does not have any code`
+
+A second, chain-identity check (not just "is my one address present") is the OP Stack
+`L1Block` predeploy, which exists on Base and every OP chain but not on L1:
+
+```bash
+cast call 0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000015 "number()(uint256)" --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+- fork of Base → a recent L1 block number, e.g. `26036031`
+- fork of Ethereum mainnet → `does not have any code`
+
+Both were run against real forks while writing this. Note that neither is fooled by
+`--chain-id 31337`, because both query *state inherited from the fork source* rather than
+the node's self-reported identity — which is exactly the property `cast chain-id` lacks.
+
+Run one of these before concluding the fork is healthy. Empty reads of a known-deployed
+mainnet-token address are a fork-target symptom far more often than a contract bug.

